════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
WHY THIS CHANGE IS BEING MADE — READ THIS FULLY
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CURRENT PROBLEM WITH AsyncTask:
The existing implementation uses napi-rs AsyncTask to run gRPC requests.
AsyncTask schedules work on libuv's thread pool — a fixed set of OS threads
Node.js uses for blocking operations. The default pool size is 4 threads.

Each AsyncTask occupies one libuv thread for the entire duration of the
request including the ~4ms network wait. With 4 threads and 4ms per request:
  4 threads × (1000ms ÷ 4ms) = ~1000 QPS maximum

This is a hard ceiling. Adding more concurrent requests beyond 4 just queues
them waiting for a libuv thread to become free. The ceiling can be raised by
setting UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE=128 as an environment variable, but this:
  - Requires customer action (not transparent on version upgrade)
  - Blocks 128 OS threads as waiting rooms for Tokio (~128MB memory)
  - Shares the pool with file I/O, DNS, crypto operations
  - Still has a ceiling at 128 concurrent requests

THE CORRECT FIX — ThreadsafeFunction + direct Tokio spawn:
Instead of scheduling work on libuv's thread pool, spawn work directly
onto the static Tokio runtime that already exists in the extension.
The V8 thread receives a Promise immediately and is never blocked.
Tokio handles the async gRPC work on its own worker threads completely
independently of libuv. When Tokio completes the network call, it uses
a ThreadsafeFunction to safely call back into the V8 thread to resolve
the Promise.

This eliminates libuv from the critical path entirely:
  - Zero libuv threads occupied during network wait
  - No ceiling from thread pool size
  - No customer action required
  - No memory overhead of blocked threads
  - Tokio's 4 worker threads handle thousands of concurrent async tasks
  - Concurrency limited only by network and Spanner capacity

ARCHITECTURE COMPARISON:

Current (AsyncTask):
  JS calls executeSqlNative()
    → napi-rs takes libuv thread from pool
      → libuv thread blocks for ~4ms waiting for Tokio
        → Tokio does actual network work
          → libuv thread released
            → resolve() called on V8 thread

Target (ThreadsafeFunction + Tokio):
  JS calls executeSqlNative()
    → napi-rs creates Promise, returns to JS immediately
      → RUNTIME.spawn() submits task to Tokio directly
        → Tokio does actual network work (no libuv involved)
          → Tokio calls tsfn.call() when complete
            → napi-rs safely wakes V8 event loop
              → V8 thread runs conversion, Promise resolves

